WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.210
We'll take a quick quick break and be back
right after this.

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:02.210
The David Pakman Show David Pakman dot com.

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.210
Welcome back to the David Pakman show today.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.890
Welcoming to the program, Barbara Freese,
who's an environmental attorney, energy analyst

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:12.269
and author of the recently released book Industrial
Strength Denial Eight Stories of Corporations

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:17.269
Defending the Indefensible From the Slave
Trade to Climate Change.

00:00:17.269 --> 00:00:19.189
Barbara, it's great to have you on today.

00:00:19.189 --> 00:00:21.439
Thanks for having me, David.

00:00:21.439 --> 00:00:24.409
So this is this is a theme, this idea of denial.

00:00:24.409 --> 00:00:26.339
It's a strategy, really.

00:00:26.339 --> 00:00:33.260
And whenever there is a disagreement in culture,
in politics, wherever, sometimes the facts

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.260
are debated.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.559
But sometimes you see a strategy of simply
simply denying the facts, saying those are

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:45.100
not the facts, or I don't believe what you
are presenting me sort of an equivalent to

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.110
bearing one's head in the sand as it may be.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:54.859
How far back does this sort of grift of denial
go as you've charted and researched it?

00:00:54.859 --> 00:01:01.300
Well, I went back as far as the slave trade
and a challenge to it in Britain in the late

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.329
seventeen hundreds and early eighteen hundreds.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:06.049
I do think of this as a part of human psychology.

00:01:06.049 --> 00:01:09.380
So I think it goes back as long as as we've
been humans.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:16.530
But as far as a documented industry campaign
of denial, we know that it's at least as old

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.780
as the slave trade.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.399
And what were the arguments made around the
slave trade?

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:24.560
They were appalling and absurd.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:32.600
They basically embraced this kind of rescue
narrative that they were not, you know, enslaving

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:37.090
people, they weren't taking captives from
Africa and putting them on horrible plantations.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.759
On the contrary, they were rescuing them from
a worse fate in Africa where they would have

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:48.390
been put to death as prisoners of war or eaten
by cannibals and delivering them to, you know,

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:53.759
first of all, on a lovely festive slave ship
to a very comfortable plantation where they

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:59.979
would be well treated and have comfortable
homes and be taken care of in in old age and

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:00.979
sickness.

00:02:00.979 --> 00:02:02.979
And it was it was really appalling.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:08.610
And because this was the British trade and
the British dominated the trade, they were

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:13.120
speaking to an audience that had no direct
experience at all of slavery.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.910
So they could really get away with with some
whoppers that they couldn't have gotten away

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:21.310
with in the United States, where people at
least saw something of slavery.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:26.770
I've even heard in the last few years that
typically from folks from certain political

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:30.950
persuasions in the United States, I'm not
for slavery.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:37.230
But conditions for some freed slaves were
worse than with certain slave owners, which

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.690
seems sort of like a similar idea.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:44.690
Well, and in fact, that was a strategy that
the British slave traders used.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:51.070
They frequently compared the conditions that
slaves lived under with the poor in Britain.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:55.810
And, of course, the poor in Britain did live
under terrible conditions.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:00.330
So it was sort of an example of sort of this
downward ratchet that that as long as there

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:05.170
were bad conditions for one group, you could
use that as an excuse to allow even worse

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.240
conditions for another group.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.760
Very present right now when we talk about
denial is climate, climate science and climate

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.290
science denial.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.070
And you write about this in the book.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.100
I've been covering it for a very long time.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:23.680
Is there something unique to climate science
denial, maybe either from a psychological

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.980
perspective or in some way that distinguishes
it from the other?

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.570
Denial grifts that you've covered?

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.680
Or is it just another version of the same
sort of thing?

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:41.300
Well, pretty much all the climate denial denials
that we see have appeared in other industries

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.290
over the decades and centuries.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:48.900
But there are some things about climate denial
that I think make it just particularly easy.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.100
One of them is that it is such a huge problem.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.310
It is such a long term problem.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:58.870
It is something that everybody contributes
to at least a little bit in terms of consuming

00:03:58.870 --> 00:03:59.870
fossil fuels.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.910
And that's such a familiar thing that our
minds are not really triggered to be afraid

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.750
of, of things that are that familiar.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:12.500
And another thing I think that makes this
unusual is just the phenomenal success of

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:13.500
climate denial.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.019
I mean, you never heard presidents of the
United States, at least in the modern era,

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:21.209
denying that tobacco caused cancer.

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:25.950
But you have heard at least one of them calling
climate change a hoax.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:33.050
Yeah, that that's interesting because some
of the same people one of the phenomena that

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:38.710
I find interesting about the climate prospect
climate issue is we know what the science

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.479
is and we know it's overwhelming.

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:45.169
And at this point, it's like almost a cliché
to repeat it time and time again.

00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:54.159
But the people that deny it seem to accept
uncritically the claims of some small sliver

00:04:54.159 --> 00:05:01.069
of scientists they can find or they in other
words, they seem to not apply the same critiques

00:05:01.069 --> 00:05:05.039
to those that happen to share their preexisting
notion.

00:05:05.039 --> 00:05:09.320
And I've not found a good way to break through
that kind of epistemological barrier when

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:10.479
I talk to those people.

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:11.479
All right.

00:05:11.479 --> 00:05:16.469
One of the things I write about in the book
is basically tribalism and its effect on the

00:05:16.469 --> 00:05:22.069
mind and how easily triggered it is and then
how incredibly powerful it is.

00:05:22.069 --> 00:05:28.620
So in times of uncertainty, in times of conflict,
it becomes particularly strong.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:35.490
And certainly the level of skepticism you
would apply to people outside your tribe becomes

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:36.490
very, very high.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:41.249
And the level of skepticism you apply to those
within your tribe, telling you what you want

00:05:41.249 --> 00:05:42.930
to hear is very, very low.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:47.800
It's also a sort of exaggerated confirmation
bias that we all indulge in personally.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:53.439
But that gets amplified in times like this
when the issue is this big and when the industry

00:05:53.439 --> 00:05:59.990
in question is really, you know, one of the
most or maybe the most powerful industry in

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:05.610
the world and where you're talking about harms
that are really inherent in its central product,

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.960
in that sense, it's kind of like tobacco.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:14.379
And, you know, interestingly, the big oil
companies now, just like Big Tobacco, don't

00:06:14.379 --> 00:06:19.449
deny anymore the fundamental facts of their
product causing harm.

00:06:19.449 --> 00:06:21.919
And yet they have been denied.

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:26.629
For so many decades and put in place so many
barriers to regulation that they're still

00:06:26.629 --> 00:06:32.970
benefiting from decades of denial passed what
seems to be effective in getting through the

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:33.970
denial?

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:36.659
I mean, I'm thinking of, you know, with coronaviruses
an example.

00:06:36.659 --> 00:06:40.969
Initially, when there were some cases in China,
it was very easy to just say it won't come

00:06:40.969 --> 00:06:41.969
here.

00:06:41.969 --> 00:06:42.969
And then it did.

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:44.810
And it's very easy to say there won't be a
lot of cases and they'll go away.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.039
And then there's lots of cases.

00:06:46.039 --> 00:06:48.690
But you say, well, we're not going to have
a lot of deaths and then there's deaths.

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:53.370
And then people will say, listen, I'm hearing
that people are dying, but I'm not seeing

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:54.370
the bodies.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:59.419
And then you see the news video of the mass
graves being dug and the refrigerated trucks.

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:05.220
And people say that's optics produced to trick
people for political reasons like there.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:11.789
There's always that ability to say it's stage
managed, it's fake, whatever.

00:07:11.789 --> 00:07:16.800
What is effective at getting past all of that?

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.520
I wish I could give you a formula that that
made it clear what's effective.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:25.430
It does seem to matter to have facts and to
have imagery and to bring that before the

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:27.620
public.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:33.710
What tends to be most effective in overcoming
denial is for there to no longer be any real

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:36.099
credible benefit to denial.

00:07:36.099 --> 00:07:41.650
So, for example, that's, I think, what brought
the tobacco industry finally into, you know,

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:45.419
the modern era, because it was to the point
where nobody believed them anyway.

00:07:45.419 --> 00:07:47.219
And they're being sued for fraud.

00:07:47.219 --> 00:07:49.219
And they had all kinds of other problems.

00:07:49.219 --> 00:07:56.050
And they realized that admitting that their
product killed people wasn't really going

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:57.580
to prevent them from selling it.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:01.599
Even after decades of saying if this was true,
we'd stop selling it.

00:08:01.599 --> 00:08:03.999
So that tends to be what happens.

00:08:03.999 --> 00:08:11.979
I I'd like to imagine that eventually when
it comes to larger efforts to overcome denial,

00:08:11.979 --> 00:08:16.680
if you think of us as being very tribal and
taking on kind of a herd mentality, then perhaps

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:22.470
when people who are at the head of the herd
switch, it might lead to some some greater

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:23.979
acceptance.

00:08:23.979 --> 00:08:29.129
You might look at the the George Floyd protests,
which isn't really the same thing.

00:08:29.129 --> 00:08:31.509
But but you had the video of his killing.

00:08:31.509 --> 00:08:32.509
Right.

00:08:32.509 --> 00:08:37.020
And I think that helped a lot of members of
the public who had no financial or ideological

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:42.010
stake in this one way or another, but except
that they wanted to believe the world was

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:44.640
no better than it is when they saw that.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:49.900
I think a certain amount of public denial
about just how bad things could be went away.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:55.731
So I do tend to be a big believer in evidence
and and trying to bring that out there, even

00:08:55.731 --> 00:08:59.380
though we do have a lot of examples of it
failing to convince people.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:05.010
How much of a problem do you see the corporate
media coverage of some of these issues being

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.010
and the spin?

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:11.190
The example I'm thinking of is although the
science on climate is, you know, ninety nine

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:17.300
point nine eight to zero point zero two or
however lopsided it is, you want to claim

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:19.560
it is, it's somewhere in that range.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:26.450
You have numerous and regular corporate media
debates where you have two people and they're

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:27.630
given equal time.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:34.810
And one person, 50 percent of the debate represents
the ninety nine point nine, eight and 50 percent

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:38.880
of the debate, meaning one person represents
the point zero two.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:44.200
It's this sort of false balance problem, which
I think corporate media engages in in order

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:47.950
to be neutral, as they say.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:49.730
But neutral is not objective.

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:54.780
And so I'm wondering how much you think that
contributes to some of this denial and provides

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:55.780
cover?

00:09:55.780 --> 00:10:00.260
Yeah, I think that has always been a problem
when it comes to particularly climate change.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:05.030
I mean, even if you were to try to do it in
some more balanced way and for example, you

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:10.680
were to have a debate with ninety nine climate
scientists who recognized the threat and one

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.640
who denied it.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.952
I think that the audience would still come
away saying, oh, well, then there are two

00:10:15.952 --> 00:10:17.780
different opinions.

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:23.820
So I think you're right that pretty much any
attention to what is now a very much discredited

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:29.370
viewpoint gives it more credibility with the
public and particularly with the public.

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:35.020
That is not scientific and not fully aware
of all the data, because you can never really

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:40.490
convey all the data or the strength of it
to a nonscientific public when it comes to

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:48.250
something like coal, which relates to the
climate debate more generally in that certainly,

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:54.710
you know, if you disbelieve the science on
climate, you might be more supportive of coal

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:58.820
because of its immediate sort of monetary
benefits and that it's cheap and we don't

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:03.150
need new infrastructure like we would with
solar or wind or whatever the case may be.

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:09.550
How have you seen or sort of observed the
coal debate happened during the current administration

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:15.190
where we've seen the president essentially
say that he wants to take take this out of

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:16.190
the market?

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:20.730
The free market, of course, pointing to letting
coal die to revitalize an industry that from

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:23.660
a climate perspective, doesn't make sense.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:27.840
Where does this fit into the discussion in
terms of people who who say, yes, we should

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:29.600
we really should bring coal back?

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:32.320
Is it a denial of the climate science?

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:38.561
Is it a sort of political or tribal based
reaction, some combination of the two?

00:11:38.561 --> 00:11:40.990
Yeah, I think it's it's certainly a combination
of the two.

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:47.110
And it's certainly a reflection of the fact
that to the extent the coal industry has been

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:51.880
defending itself in the past by saying that
you should let the market decide.

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:52.880
Right.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:57.870
That sort of ideological justification for
burning coal really wasn't what was ever motivating

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:58.870
them.

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:04.230
And in fact, what the president has tried
to do is to get power companies to buy coal

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:07.540
and subsidized coal even when they would prefer
not to.

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:15.420
So so that was a pretty clear illustration
that the ideological justification was never.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.030
Really, what was driving it?

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:21.380
The last thing I want to touch a touch on
is I think if we didn't mention this, we would

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:27.260
be missing a huge piece of this, which is
the denial side is often very well-funded.

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:30.370
And when you think back to tobacco and I forget
it.

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:34.920
What if it was the Clean Skies or Clean Air
Initiative or whatever, which was basically

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:41.190
about convincing restaurant owners, hey, if
you want freedom from regulation, we need

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:45.500
you on board so that people will be allowed
to smoke inside your restaurants when the

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:48.670
Movement for No Smoking in restaurant started.

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:56.470
These are really well-funded movements and
that seems to dramatically skew a lot of the

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:58.010
conversations that are happening.

00:12:58.010 --> 00:12:59.010
Does it not?

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.010
Oh, sure.

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:05.750
I mean, the industries that I wrote about
and I focus just on eight industries and most

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:07.790
of the really big ones.

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:16.650
They had enormous amounts of money to promote
their viewpoint, to give to other free market

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:22.290
ideological groups, to promote their views
more secretly, to hire people, to write articles,

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:25.490
but not to explain where they got the money.

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:31.770
And then ultimately, you end up with an entire
industry that supports other industries that

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:34.350
are trying to be tried to deny the science.

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:40.660
So you've got that the law firms, the PR firms,
the advertising agencies and these so-called

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:44.880
nonprofits that that become advocates to oppose
regulation.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:51.010
So, I mean, I try to write about this partly
from a kind of psychological perspective,

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:52.170
looking at the social science.

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:59.560
And I think what happens is that for the individuals
in the corporations that confront these charges,

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:04.580
that there is a very natural, instinctive
inclination to deny it, which is just part

00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:05.880
of human nature.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:10.980
I think it then moves pretty quickly into
a corporate strategy and then it moves from

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:17.030
there into its own kind of a denial industry
with the same PR firms and others appearing

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:18.240
again and again.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:21.010
And then I think it moves into an ideology.

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:28.490
And so it's that progression that makes denial,
this very human thing, but but promoted by

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:32.890
these very powerful institutions, such a destructive
thing.

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:38.760
Do you have any insights into the psychology
of the cogs in the wheel, so to speak, that

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:41.260
are carrying out a lot of these that these
denial campaigns?

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:46.250
Because I could imagine a situation where
the people working at the PR firm or the people

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:50.520
working at a corporation or the law firm at
the individual level.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:53.700
Many of them probably know that what they're
doing is lying and that they're denying what

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:55.000
what what are the facts?

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:56.460
Is it as simple as.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:58.050
Listen, I'm a cog in the wheel.

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:00.140
I'm just trying to put food on the table.

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:02.460
And this is going to happen regardless.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:06.520
Or what have you had any insights into that
at the individual level?

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:09.520
I think that is definitely part of it.

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:14.940
And corporations themselves, especially the
big ones younger that are designed to make

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:19.080
the individual not feel responsible for what
the corporation does.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:20.830
It does that through division of labor.

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:22.320
You're just a cog in the wheel.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:27.550
And we know for sure that diminishes your
impure feelings of responsibility for the

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:28.550
consequences.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:31.960
You've got management divided from ownership.

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:36.690
You've got this ideology that tells managers
that their only responsibility is to maximize

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:39.770
shareholder pressure and shareholder profit.

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:43.130
Meanwhile, shareholders don't feel responsible
because they're far away.

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:44.130
It's transactional.

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:48.690
They may just own the stock for a little while
and they don't know what's going on anyway.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:54.200
We also know that power tends to focus the
mind on your most salient goals, which, if

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.920
you're at a corporate executive, is profit.

00:15:56.920 --> 00:16:02.910
It also, there's some evidence seems to reduce
your risk awareness and your risk perception

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:07.180
once you become aware of it and your general
social responsibility.

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:13.020
Again, there's the tribalism, the competitiveness
inherent in incorporations, the market mindset.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:16.640
So, yeah, there's there are a lot of psychological
things you could point to.

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:21.200
In fact, I suggest in the book that if you
are a supervillain trying to get the world

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:27.690
to destroy itself by taking crazy risks, you
might just create a society very much like

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:34.320
our corporate dominated market society, because
there are many, many things there that undermine

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:38.810
our innate sense of social responsibility.

00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:39.810
Very well said.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:45.660
The book is Industrial Strength Denial Eight
Stories of Corporations Defending the Indefensible

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:47.480
from the Slave Trade to Climate Change.

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:49.750
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Barbara.

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:52.130
Barbara, thank you so much for your time today.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:53.130
Thank you, David.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:54.130
I appreciate it.

